// The first procedure here that reads a geometry it is not running over.
//
// **`uses far : Geometry` says this L2 takes a second geometry.** `L2 :
// Geometry -> Geometry` is an endomorphism, and the two ways to break it are
// now both spelled: `amplify` breaks it on the *count* axis, one element in and
// several out, and this breaks it on the *arity*.
//
//   karakuri-cli --set lattice_shell.kir,sphere_shell.kir,morph.kir,soft_points.kir \
//                --edge morph.far=sphere_shell
//
// **`far` is this file's own name for what it takes**, exactly as `consumes
// position` names an attribute without naming which L1 supplies it. Which
// geometry fills it is the Set's answer and is written in the Set — an `edge`
// record, or `--edge` on the way in. That is what keeps this file a library
// part: a `.kir` that named a node would be coupled to one Set and could be
// used in no other. An unbound slot is refused rather than filled in from
// whatever geometry happens to be lying around.
//
// **The correspondence is the slot index**, so element 5 of one geometry
// becomes element 5 of the other. That is only the same element in both while
// nothing moves one, which is why both sources must be static — no `spawn`, no
// `kill()` — and why the Set refuses the pair rather than matching each element
// with a stranger.
//
// **The far geometry is never drawn on its own.** A Set with a bound slot is
// one source made of two simulations, with one chain and one set of renderers
// over it, so "is the far end also on screen?" is a question with no place to
// be asked.
//
// One `consumes` covers both sides: this node reads the same attribute from
// each, so declaring it once is declaring it for both.

proc morph {
  kind L2
  uses far : Geometry

  // A fader, a signal binding, a transition and a published control all reach
  // it, because it is an ordinary `param` — the same argument `weight` makes
  // one layer down.
  param k : float [0.0, 1.0] = 0.0

  consumes position, tint

  deform {
    // `mix` here is componentwise, so the blend factor is widened rather than
    // passed as a scalar: this language's `mix` takes three of one type.
    let w = vec3(k, k, k);

    position = mix(position, far.position, w);
    tint     = mix(tint, far.tint, w);
  }
}
